imes it's  programmable like in a declarative sense  you can do a lot of non-trivial things  with the data structure that the data  scientists have access to so the last  little piece I'm going to talk about in  the implementation details here so  there's an additional data layer we've  provided now the atomic has its own data  layer about the things that are going  into it and that's the atomic schema and  it tracks information like which things  are references which things are strings  and so on which things have to be unique  across all entities at which things can  point are you know many references so  they can refer to many other things and  which fields can only refer to singular  values we've added an additional layer  on top of this that enforces our own  constraints and provides leverage for  the things we want it to and that's  called the metamodel and the metamodel  is basically schema annotations as data  and atomic so we track for instance what  kinds of things are in the database  which things refer to each other is  there some kind of component or parent  relationship between those things and if  so what direction is it and this kind of  model derives the structure of the  config so if you look at this  configuration file here we see data set  pointing to subjects which points to  therapies which has a field that points  to you know how to map an ID about those  things the structure of this file and  these different details sorry this is  all determined by the metamodel and  data-driven based on the current  contents of the atomic database it's not  explicitly programmed in code so that  theoretically that means that you could  adapt this to different domains if  enough of the data shape was similar in  our context it means we're able to  operate  where we iterate very quickly on schema  changes because we don't have to rewrite  a bunch of new code to do it you just  have to update the schema contents and  then that drives the rest of the ETL  process so it turns out this is you know  a very general system now sorry I think  I talked about everything I was supposed  to talk about on the slide on the last  one so I'll go ahead and skip that so  there are a few things that are kind of  more schema specific this is all about  downstream validation in this case it  uses a combination of de Tomic queries  and closure spec to ensure that there's  you know referential integrity between  things that all the values make sense  that things are within some biologically  plausible range a common error that this  catches is somebody scaled something to  a different set of values and they were  supposed to and so we catch these kinds  of things fairly quickly and somebody  goes back and fixes the data and this  kind of thing again this enables you  know we have pretty printers for errors  and so on and the tools that when people  see these problems they can iterate very  quickly on getting back and getting the  data right and you don't discover that  the analysis is wrong sometime later or  worst case after the papers published  right we're you know well ahead of that  now in terms of what you actually have  to do command line wise as a data  scientist to make use of this the  command line workflow is pretty simple  we have a command prepare transact and  validate we also have the concept of  doing this against a staging database  first and then if all of that works and  the validations pass then you can push  it into the production database and you  know the total set of arguments is  pretty low you have to know about a  working directory which is where  transaction data gets created and you  have to know about add atomic config  which tells you how to talk to a  particular de Tomek database and again  for the production database workflow you  have to log in and go through you know  oh ah handshake and then be able to push  your working directory once the  transaction data is definitely good up  so it can get incorporated after review  into a production database  okay so from here I'm going to turn it  back to lacy so I've talked about the  technical details of how this works  she's going to go through a real example  and real analysis case  so as been described are dated virtually  and in fact are scheming agnostic except  for the validation component one of the  powerful assurances candle is that we  have created a unified schema for  answering or therapy data and I won't go  through the details of the schema but  another powerful part of it is that in  the schema and also a reference data  that we've loaded into the database we  have domain semantics with ontologies  for genus protein cell types diseases  drugs adverse events there are a lot of  pathologies in this space and we've  tried to connect to as many of them as  possible and we have the ability to add  corners together so to walk through a  specific example I wanted to tell you  quickly about one of those clinical  trials that we've run at the Parker  Institute so this clinical trial is  called prints which stands for something  very long it's a clinical trial in  pancreatic cancer which is a really high  need area and the clinical trial  combines chemotherapy with a novel type  of immunotherapy so in pancreatic cancer  with the baseline chemotherapy in this  study the overall response rate so that  means the fraction of patients who in  this therapy see a significant shrinkage  of their tumors is 23% from the large  phase 3 study that had this combination  approved in our study is it's only been  very small so far and this data is only  from 24 patients but right now we're  seeing a response rate after phase 1 B  of 54 percent so overall this is much  improved from basic chemotherapy  response rates and even more excitingly  in many of our patients who have been on  therapy now for a year year and a half  we're seeing a durable continued  response so we're following up on this  right now in a larger phase 2 study but  I wanted to tell you about some of our  analysis on the phase one and how candle  was incorporated so at the Parker  Institute one of our missions is  to learn as much as we can biologically  from every clinical trial that we've run  so on all of our patients for this trial  we collected tumor and blood samples and  we ran a large array of biological  assays on the samples ranging from tumor  immune imaging to sequencing and  cytometry and cell-free DNA in the blood  and more and then we combined all of  this with the clinical data from these  patients and we loaded it all into  Candle so the steps in doing this is  first to take all of this data and  process it into a useful tabular format  so if you think back to that what is the  data scientist job look like that I  talked about in the beginning first you  have to get it into a table of  measurements and this we do using a lot  of other infrastructure that we've built  at the Parker Institute and external  tools that I won't talk about today but  once we have it all in useful table  format that's when we prepare config and  we can load it into Candle so this is I  know you can't read this but this is a  simplified version of what the import  config looks like for this data set so  zooming in on specific parts so the very  top level is just information about the  import it's telling me who's importing  it where can I find my mappings file  what's the name of the import next we  get into the actual biological data so  as Ben said the structure of the config  is directly driven by the structure of  the schema and metamodel so we have our  top level data set entity which has  attributes of name you can see the name  of the study and then assays and the  assays are where the actual biological  data comes in so here we have an assay  measuring cell free DNA we have an  attribute with its technology  description and in this assay we have a  couple measurements sets and when we get  down here to actual measurements the  data is no longer written directly into  the config but is instead fed in through  a file so this is a data file that we've  gotten by processing the data it tells  me for each sample what is a cell-free  DNA measurement in it and you can see  that I have the input file here and then  columns in the input file on the right  map to attributes on the measurement  entity on the left and you'll notice  that each measurement references a  sample entity so the next part in the  config is the actual sample data so this  is also fed in with an input file with  one line per sample entity and  information about the sample like what  patient it came from what visit it came  from etc and this is where Pretz  knowledge of the meta model of the  schema and reference resolution magic  kind of come in so up here I had in my  measurements file references to samples  right but pret needs to turn this into  from IDs in a file in a column to  attributes on entities that reference  other attributes so the sample entity on  measurement is actually a reference to a  sample entity so prêt takes care of  resolving that identifying which sample  entity I'm referring to and then  inserting that as data into the database  and from there we go on to the rest of  the config which houses the clinical  data all of the other assay data the  other biological data such as  information about the subjects and what  therapies they received and pret has a  lot of other machinery for dealing with  things like missing data and reverse  references and a lot of other details  we're not going into so now we've  processed the data we've made the config  and as Ben mentioned the steps to  importing data are preparing and then  transacting and then validating so as a  user my experience is that first I run  prepare and then I'm going to get back a  bunch of errors that are gonna tell me  you know this range is supposed to be  zero to one and you told me it was 77 or  you know this gene needs to look like  this form