s providing tooling so that  they can do queries and our mostly we  hide the fact that it's de Tomic from  them but one area where this is  different is we believe that data log  provides a lot of power and simplicity  to their workflow so we want to give  them access to use the atomic data log  directly in the our environment and the  way we do that is by serializing queries  over plain JSON data now if you've  looked at des Tomic before you probably  see in various different queries there  are a lot of different you know where  clauses different values that show up  here and there  the most important thing that we have to  sort of disambiguate on a parser you  know lexer pass or however you want to  think about it is most of these cases  aren't that bad right if it starts with  a question mark its variable if it  starts with a call and it's a keyword  and most of the time you know you don't  have real literal data that starts with  those things and if you do you can  provide some kind of escape you do have  to have some structural awareness so  that if you have a plain string that's  supposed to be a function call or an  aggregate or something you can  disambiguate that from a constant  literal it turns out you can do this  with spec and specs own collection  oriented regular expressions and you can  just take the BNF like grammar from the  diatomic query Docs and turn that into  your parser and that's what I did in  this case so you have a plain JSON way  of serializing queries to a service that  then runs them in there and you said you  don't have any additional dependencies  on the our side so using stuff from our  directly this is what it looks like  you've got a very kind of lightweight  thing that looks like just using des  Tomic and not passing a plain string  query or something like that to it so  you can actually you know do substantive  you know feedback or validation on the  query before you send it if you want to  this has enabled a lot of downstream  